<<~ ahu slot=`#/purpose`>>

!! The controller's handoff ~ one seat, passed in the open

A session that holds the CONTROLLER's seat drives the build toward the roadmap's attractor (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-attractor`>>): it briefs lanes, routes their handbacks, pushes green heads, keeps canon and the changelog true, and brings the operator only what changes authority, custody at rest or identity bytes. This carrier holds what a session needs to take that seat: the practice, the state, and the way two live sessions pass the seat while both stand.

Read with it, in order: the boot seed (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot`>>), the roadmap's cold start (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/cold-start`>>), then #/the-lane-law.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seat`>>

!! The seat ~ what the controller does

* ''It briefs lanes.'' One lane per feature path, each led by one lead whose lineage the brief names (`mixed-model-swarm#/the-seats`, `#/the-tiers`), its crew standing as `mixed-model-swarm#/the-shapes` names. A lead's brief stands as a file, and the lead writes a resume sheet beside it.
* ''It keeps up to five lane slots filled'' (the sixth worktree holds the train). At every resume it reads `ListAgents` against the road: a lead whose seat vanished relaunches from its resume sheet and brief, and a slot that frees fills from the roadmap's order. The controller lands a one-line CI cure itself at most; a second cure means the lane's lead relaunches. A brief stays short and points at the shared law; it names the lane's ground, the ground it leaves alone, its first line of handback, and where it stops.
* ''It routes handbacks'' and owns one watcher per seat. A handback answers with one status line and plain notes. A finding on another lane's ground travels to that lane as a message or a patch.
* ''It lands batches.'' A lane pushes its own branch once per feature. The controller reads the lane's CI and held-out on the head (`node tools/ci-verdict.mjs --head <sha> --branch <branch>`: the full CI workflow, the container drivers of `held-out.yml`, and the shards where a bisect ran; it answers GREEN, RED or UNREADABLE, `--wait` reads until neither workflow stands unread), and lands each green lane through the native merge queue on `develop`. A lane push runs the Quick gates and the cells its diff touches (the scope reads the touched packages and their in-repo dependents, the witness cells where canon moved beside code, and the full battery for anything it cannot classify; operator, 2026-11-19, beside the 2026-11-18 doorstep ruling): its head reads green on those while the e2e gate walks its one doorstep step, and a lane run never reads as a land, and the controller dispatches the whole battery on a lane head by hand (`gh workflow run CI --ref <branch>`) when the land wants it before the queue: `gh pr merge <pr> --merge --auto` enters the lane's pull request once its checks read, the queue's `merge_group` run reads it on top of every lane queued ahead, and on green the queue moves `develop` to that exact sha (`mixed-model-swarm#/the-lanes`). ''The required checks stand in one place'': `REQUIRED_CHECKS` in `tools/ci-graph.mjs` names the four (`Quick gates`, `Test`, `Witnesses`, `E2E / Merge shard reports`), and the repository ruleset on `develop` MUST name that same list. Canon goes direct once its pins read green; a direct code push stands as the emergency path past the queue, under the ruleset's bypass. A red reading routes to the lane whose ground it touches; an UNREADABLE reading waits and reads again, and counts as neither green nor red. `tools/ci-verdict.mjs` reads CI no closer than three minutes apart: the house pace, `POLL_FLOOR_S` (180 s) in `tools/ci-verdict.mjs`, set by the operator's law of 2026-11-05 after the GitHub API quota ran out. `ci-verdict --interval` widens it, a value under it refuses by name (`poll-under-floor: 30 < 180`), and no environment variable moves it; the pace decides only when the tool reads again, never a verdict.
* ''It keeps the record.'' The change record rides each lane commit's own trailers (`Breaking:`, `Changed:`, `Amends: <sha>`; merge commits carry none), and `node tools/changelog-render.mjs <from>..<to>` renders it along `develop`'s first parent; no hand edits a changelog file, and an operator act lives in the house as a record of its own. Road commits ride behind a batch. Canon true-ups by a read-only collision of the roadmap against the tree, then hand-applied high-confidence lines.
* ''It settles forks down the ladder'' `working-procedures#/forks` names, and takes a house reading, marks it as one, and builds. A parked item stays parked; a turn ends on an act taken.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-practice`>>

!! The practice ~ what every lane keeps

* ''Talk-story flows'': turn Mu first, then user stories, then carry only surviving forks with a lean; where none survive, flow into OODA-HA loops. A question stalls nothing unless it blocks the work; correct an overturned ruling plainly.
* ''Browser and CI watches'': browser-mode runs go headless (`lararium-web`'s vitest config on b1-lift). A watcher polls completion; before #175 lands it never uses `ci-verdict --wait` on a lane branch. A push changing only canon paths (`tools/ci-ignored-paths.json`) runs CI's doorstep set alone (`Scope`, `Quick gates`, the e2e doorstep step), so its full battery stands on its parent's run.

* ''Write a land message before the wait, never inside it.'' A watcher that writes its land message only on the green path leaves no file on red; the next seat then finds none (row7-s3b and PR #172, 2026-11-06).
* ''A refused command stops the lane.'' Never retry it, reshape it, split it, switch tools for it, or hand it to another spirit. The lane reports it; the controller surfaces it to the operator. A sidestep that reaches the same end counts as a retry.
* ''Edits happen in the lane's own worktree,'' committed there on the lane branch with `git commit -F <msg> --only -- <paths>` after `git status --short` and `git diff HEAD -- <path>` show only the lane's lines. One write per command line. No inline script writes into the main tree. A lane worktree leaves by a plain `git worktree remove` after its census; one that initialized submodules refuses a plain remove, so the lane proves its files landed and asks the operator for the `--force`.
* ''Never `git stash`'' in any form, `git stash list` included; never `--no-verify`; never a foreground `sleep`, alone or chained: a long run goes in the foreground with a long timeout, or in the background with its completion notice.
* ''Red first, with a control''; a race gets pinned by the product's own event, never by a sleep, a retry loop or load. A load probe lives only as an opt-in triage script under `tools/`.
* ''The plugin bundle and genesis move only by the controller's word.'' A lane that changes `packages/lararium-tw5/` source or tiddlers rebuilds the bundle in the same commit and declares the gap in `genesis/unbaked.json`; one labelled `GENESIS RE-BAKE` folds every declared entry. That re-bake (`build:genesis`) re-cuts code and carries the founding floor forward; only a `main` release's bake (`build:genesis-release`) re-cuts the floor from canon (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/node/genesis-island#/the-floor-rides-releases`>>).
* ''Scratch roots alone'' for founding, standing or unlocking (`tools/test-scratch-marker.mjs`, then `tools/seed-test-custody.mjs`); never the operator's homes; never a real passphrase, domain or credential.
* ''After a machine restart'' the session scratchpad empties and every worktree under it vanishes. Durable state lives in git and in the session folder; a resumed lane re-reads its resume sheet, provisions a fresh worktree at HEAD, and rebuilds.
* ''O-takiage'' closes every lane and every settle as `mixed-model-swarm#/the-otakiage` names it.
* ''Fetch before you act, and again before you push.'' Another lane can merge into the work branch between two turns; a lane that pushes past it, or reasons from a stale origin, cures the wrong tree.
* ''Two witnesses ride CI alone.'' `tools/tree-clean-witness.sh` and `browser-weld-witness` drive e2e boots, so a lane brief names them as CI's and no lane runs either locally.
* ''A local green proves the tree it read.'' A worktree whose `node_modules` link into another checkout tests that checkout's build; every vitest config refuses a stale or foreign `dist` (`tools/vitest-dist-guard.mjs`). Provision lane worktrees with `tools/provision-worktree.mjs <empty-dir>`, and run the heavy battery through the battery lock (`mixed-model-swarm#/the-battery`).
* ''One red in CI masks every package after it.'' `pnpm test` runs the packages in order and stops at the first red, so a cured red can reveal the next. Before pushing a cure, run the packages that come after it, or read the next CI run whole.
* ''Read the containers' own logs before naming a cause.'' A held-out cell uploads each container's log as an artifact (`gh run download <run> -n <cell>`); tallying each container's own lines split the carriage red where the witness's verdict could not. An in-process reproduction holds one Repo, so it cannot show a defect that lives between a CLI process and a running daemon.
* ''Give a sandboxed spirit the logs it cannot fetch.'' A Codex build or read seat reaches no network; fetch the failing job logs and hand it the excerpt as a file.
* ''A review lands nothing until a second face reads it'' (`mixed-model-swarm#/the-lens-law`): a cheap eye's clean read clears nothing, and a tensioned finding gets measured by running it.
* ''Launch background spirits through the harness's background mode'', never a plain `&`: a plain `&` brings no completion notice, and `pgrep -f <pattern>` matches its own shell's command line.
* ''Shell traps in zsh'': `$R:t` reads as a path modifier, so write `${R}:path`; an unquoted variable holding several paths does not split (use `${(f)VAR}`); a `grep` pattern starting with `<<^` treats the caret as an anchor (use `grep -F`); `rm` on a path built from variables trips the safety check, so remove by literal path.
* ''A corrupt object heals by the operator's hand.'' `git fsck --full` names it; rebuild that pack in a scratch repo from an intact working copy and verify it there (run inside the damaged repo, `verify-pack`'s collision check reads the corrupt copy); the swap into `.git/objects` stays the operator's act.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lane-law`>>

!! The lane law ~ what every lead's brief points at, so a session on any machine reads it here

A session reads this carrier and the roadmap alone; no note outside the repository binds a lane. A lead's brief stays short, names its ground, the ground it leaves alone and where it stops, and points here.

!!! The crew
* ''The crew's seats, shapes and launches'' stand at `mixed-model-swarm`: which lineage holds which seat (#/the-seats), the local and cloud crews (#/the-shapes), the lens law, the Copilot seat's read-only role and budget, and the Codex and Copilot launch lines (#/the-launch).
* A lane hands back ONCE, short, in the shape `mixed-model-swarm#/the-shapes` names: one `<<~ status "…">>` line, the head, the CI and held-out readings, the story and its walk, rulings taken from canon with sources, true operator asks.

!!! Git
* A git command that writes runs alone on its line. Never `git stash` in any form, `git stash list` included (a PreToolUse hook, `tools/stash-guard.mjs`, refuses any Bash call that reaches it, and `.codex/rules/stash.rules` forbids its prefix to Codex); never `--no-verify`; never touch `.git/config`; never push the span (`develop`, the integration branch) or `main`, since a lane pushes its own lane branch; never `git worktree remove --force` on a lane worktree, since a bench leaves only after its census proves every file it holds landed.
* A lead works in its own worktree (`node tools/provision-worktree.mjs <empty-dir>` provisions one: inputs copied, `node_modules` copied out of the pnpm store, never hardlinked, so the lane shares no writable inode with the store, the main checkout or another lane (the provision refuses by name a module file carrying a second link), every package built and stamped, no link left pointing into the main tree; the test door binds every `node_modules` read-only (its build stamps alone writable), the install seat binds the store (its index opened frozen) and the pnpm metadata cache read-only, and no self-test builds into the checkout it runs from), and checks `git config core.bare` reads `false` before and after.
* A lead commits on its lane branch in its own worktree after `pnpm build`: it confirms the built CLI answers, reads `git status --porcelain -- <paths>` and `git diff -- <paths>` showing only its lines, then commits with `git commit -F <msg> --only -- <paths>`. It never commits or reverts a line another hand wrote. A subject reads `lar:///ha.ka.ba/<pkg-or-bag>/<area>/<w1.w2.w3> — plain summary`; `-F`, never `-m`.

!!! Refusals and waiting
* A refused or denied command stops that step. Never retry it, reshape it, split it, switch tools for it or hand it to another spirit; save the work as a patch and hand back at once. The controller surfaces it to the operator.
* No foreground `sleep`, alone or chained. A long run goes in the foreground with a long timeout, or in the background with its completion notice.

!!! The battery
* One named e2e file whose own waits exceed the cap runs locally under `timeout 600`, still headless in the bwrap PID namespace with the 1536 MB heap; every other run keeps `timeout 180`, and no whole suite runs locally (operator, 2026-11-06: //"Approve the 600s e2e cap, OODA-HA."//). The 180 s CI poll floor separately governs GitHub reads.
* A lane's bench gate runs on the floor before it lands, as the familiar's step and never a git hook: `node tools/floor-run.mjs -- node tools/bench-gate.mjs` runs the familiar's lane checks over the lane's touched packages with develop merged in (the fast witnesses, the carrier pins, the tool tests and each touched package's suites), so the sum of lanes reads green before any lane joins it; it runs every step and refuses by name (`BENCH GATE REFUSES: <step>`, exit 1), and a lane cures the named step before it lands.
* One battery per batch, run through the battery lock as `mixed-model-swarm#/the-battery` orders it; one e2e file runs locally and the whole sets ride the lane branch's CI. A red CI run gets bisected (a `ci/<lane>` push per suspect head, the first red named between a proven green and a red), never guessed. A live commit says so in its message and ends a batch only where later work builds on its bytes.
* Suites run from source under a scratch `HOME` and a scratch `LAR_ROOT`, with `LAR_BAGS` unset. Never `pnpm install` in the main tree. Before landing a test file, the charter and gate-dial funnels and the unbuilt-laws witness run.
* No standing test code produces load; a race gets pinned by a handshake between the parties.
* A test that spawns or signals a process runs inside a bwrap PID namespace (`bwrap --unshare-pid --die-with-parent --dev-bind / / --proc /proc --`), under `NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=1536` and `timeout 180`, with no fake pid 0 or 1: a group kill refuses any pgid at or below 1 (`killGroup` in `tools/launch-codex.mjs`), and a witness reads any `process.kill` outside it as red. Daemon-side verbs (`packages/lararium-node`, keyhive) run single test files only; CI runs the suites.

!!! Canon and genesis
* Each commit carries its canon lines; law lives in one carrier and the roadmap points at it. Prose keeps L-Prime and stays evergreen. A carrier re-stamps with `lares meme normalize <file>`, one file per call, never piped. Identity keys in a first fence never change. Never edit `bags/elyncia*`, `ftls`, `sdm`, `wtf`, the boot seed, or the submission-guarded carriers.
* A change that moves a plugin bundle rebuilds and commits the bundle and declares the gap in `genesis/unbaked.json`; only a labelled `GENESIS RE-BAKE` folds it, on the controller's word. A step that would move bytes a genesis artifact carries stops and reports which bytes move.
* Custody, vault and sealed writes count as destroy-class: scratch roots alone, synthetic names and keys only, never the operator's homes.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-cloud-lanes`>>

!! The cloud lanes ~ the briefs a cloud session takes by name

A cloud session opens on branch `develop`, stands the house from the boot seed, reads the roadmap's #/the-attractor, #/cold-start and #/rulings, this carrier whole (#/the-lane-law binds it), `working-procedures`, and its lane's kit, then walks its lane below. It leads as the Episkopos over the cloud crew `mixed-model-swarm#/the-shapes` names.

A cloud lane commits on its OWN branch, `develop--<lane>`, cut from `develop`, and pushes that branch alone, once per feature (`mixed-model-swarm#/the-feature-unit`); CI fires on it (held-out runs per train, on `develop`), and the lane reads both with `node tools/ci-verdict.mjs --head <sha> --branch <branch>` (through the GitHub connector where the container's `gh` holds no login). The committed `.claude/settings.json` runs `tools/stash-guard.mjs` before every Bash call; a refusal from it stops that step. It never pushes `develop` or `main`: the controller lands each lane branch in a batch (`mixed-model-swarm#/the-lanes`). It merges `develop` into its lane branch once, right before its push, where a conflict asks. Its hand-back lists the canon and changelog lines for the roadmap and `CHANGELOG.md`, and the lane edits neither. It stops at a clean boundary when context runs short and hands back in the lane law's shape, with its lane branch and head.

!!! Lane `wire-socket` ~ past lane
Train W's assembled cutover landed as PR #44: every vessel socket opens through the sealed face gate on one handshake, fleet join serves every face, a fresh browser device stands at the floor, and a herm answers the knock where a Nexus carries it. The roadmap's `W` items name what stays owed.

!!! Lane `carriage-fold` ~ a local session's lane
Kit: <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/carriage-fold-lane`>>. Road item `W18`: the carrier folds walk the roll anchors (PR #50, merged 2026-10-19), and `P20`'s gate half reads a place contract across a roll (PR #54), so held-out reads green on every driver; DreamDom-Wright builds `F6` on lane `holder-owner`, owing the deliberate cross-epoch held-out cell beside it. A lane under `mixed-model-swarm#/the-lanes`: its own sub-branch, the work swarm's build pair in one worktree, the other lineage's read, the overwatch, a pull request.

!!! Lane `browser-custody` ~ past lane
Its custody API landed (the roadmap's `B1` item names what it built and what stays owed). Its boot caller rides with whichever lane next holds `open-browser-vessel.ts` and the web boot. The persona roots' seal waits built and red-first on `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5--persona-root-seal`: the root mint refuses `no-custody-slot`, so it merges only after that boot binds a slot and unlocks before `openBrowserVessel`.

!!! Lane `kahu-plane` ~ past lane
Train P after N landed: the chartered classes, exact-bytes promotion, Kahu-cabal admission, the co-signed steward map, the re-anchoring fence and the anchored ancestry count, with R-KAHU witnessed across two vessels. The crossroads crossing asks the seats (`P21` built). The roadmap's `P18` to `P20` name what stays owed. `P18` rides with whichever lane holds both vessel composers. `P20`'s carrier-fold half stands through PRs #50 and #54 (`carriageAncestryFromBoard`, with the writer cure); `P19`'s cabal half waits parked at the tag `archive/gate-ancestry` (`eb53f266e`), its commit message naming the writer and verifier cure.

!!! Lane `scout` ~ past report
The dated `next-path-scout.md` report records the earlier tree; its top line names the commits that supersede its stale observations.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/state`>>
!! State ~ the controller trues this section at every handoff

As of the night of 2026-11-15, origin holds `a40f3dc06` (pushed on the operator's word; CI 40 of 40 green on pull request #218) and the image stamped from it stands on the hearth, unlocked by the operator. Above it the main checkout holds wave two's first batch, unpushed: road item `L4f` (the hearth's daemon working document crosses to the face's own page), `L42a` (the rite walker and its receipt), `C1`'s first slice (the node legs' face-and-Nexus aliases, the sibling peer id by face), and two cures from the batch's cold read (the census door on `hearthDaemonOf`; a moved working slot shares nowhere). The road's <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-order`>> ranks every open item into three bands (the re-founding, wave two, after), and its #/the-leaps and #/stride-0-hearth hold the items (`C1` to `C16`, the `L17` phases, `L35` to `L44`). A cold controller reads that section, then this one; the live session state rides the daemon wiki's memory memes (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/state/`).

* ''What stands on the span'': the veil floor and the leaf fleet; the contract build (one workbench for a wiki on a vessel, one folder for a workbench, every carry act following the document); wave one (the veil's first cut, the browser's confirm gate, the one family law with the orphan folder and its flag, the tiddler door on the anchor with its delete's two modes and the port's paged filter, the carry residue, the recipe and delete law in canon); and the wake lane (the wake reads the memory index and a compaction hook carries the memes in play, road `L43`; one tend aligns every host's hooks, road `L44`). The road's #/the-leaps names each in one line, and the CHANGELOG holds the detail.
* ''The hearth'': the operator founded it on 2026-11-14 from the image at `d9c96f525`, wearing the steward (face 0) beside the engineer (face 1); the MCP seat answers. The handoff walk wrote six `stage/index` memes into the steward's daemon wiki through the meme door (house laws, two rulings, open ledger, standing orders, where things live), and the wake reads them back, before and after a container restart; the operator minted `works`. The operator's `lares home tend` ran twice (the first lifted the ingest hook from three hosts and seated the compaction hook; the second wrote nothing), so `L44`'s act stands done, while one real `PreCompact` and the wake after it stay owed (`L43`, `L44`). The operator's running image predates the CI-Mender's cures.
* ''The merge stands held'' by the operator, pending a fresh browser walk on the new image. The first live walk's browser leg broke on a reload (the persona-KEL chain stood absent from the local board, the Binding Gate failed closed, and the page booted as its own island where the join booted admitted); a joined browser and a joined node each boot again on the island the landing seeded (`b868d3799`, `eb9133f52`; red then green in `tests/joined-face-reload-island.test.ts` and `tests/joined-node-restart-island.test.ts`), and no live browser has walked the cure. The image at the head, the push on the operator's word and that walk stay the controller's lane. The walk's other finds ride `C15` and `C17`, and two node shapes of the same island reading stay owed (the road's #/the-leaps, the owed items riding the leaps).
* ''The burn-and-sweep pass'' stands landed (operator, 2026-11-14: //"Pono, but burn the `retired` stuff, early-alpha no backwards compatibility needed, o-takiage/aftermaths sweeps needed on this & the recent work."//; `c907eaef1` to the span's head): the house owns its hooks folder, so the reality tunnel derives which host hook commands `tend` lifts from its `[[home]]` entries alone and enumerates none; the road, the runbook's carry block and the span's canon read what stands; the names no caller read left the code; the genesis floor carries all of it. No lane runs.
* ''The next moves'', in order: the operator's browser walk on the restood image (a join, two reloads, then `L4f`'s walk under `L4p`); the push of the batch on the operator's word and CI on #218; the restart onto the batch's image and its unlock; the operator's QA pass and the merge to `main`; then wave two's next items. One note for every walk: a wear that refuses with port-silence while builders load the machine succeeds on a retry at idle (road `C16`).
* ''Wave two'' (the road's ranks 6 to 13): `C1`'s second cut (the face-scoped adapter on each dial and gate leg); `C2` the realm's key (the rest gate, the roster's wrap) with `C15` the joined face's workbench seat beside it; `C9` the ceremony channel and key zeroing; `C10` the host reset gate (an offline CLI proof verb by a spirit, then the controller alone wires `tools/lares-container.sh` and its `.ps1`); the first three things beside them (`L42` the rite script and test ledger, `L40` the code map, `L41` the work as tiddlers); `L17-2a` the relay, `L17-2b` the `act` and `catalog` asks, `L17-2c` the meme door's remainder, `L17-2d` the road's items as tiddlers. From wave two on, each builder runs the suites of the packages it touched, a join runs every suite once, and a brief takes the lane card's shape.
* ''Standing orders'' (operator, 2026-11-14): //"You have my full confidence, enact all & launch parallel spirits (relaunching them when needed) in flowstate once the Wright lands its work."// It covers no push before a passing cold rehearsal, no `lares reset`, `found` or `up` on the real hearth (the operator's terminal), nothing in `~/.lares`, and no port 4321. The TiddlyWiki caps ride as a priority lane (`L17`), phased: //"I want maybe 1 or 2 more full parallel waves of work done before I refound & we dogfood directly from inside the lararium again."//
* ''Briefs say where a worktree starts'': the harness seats an agent's worktree on `main`, so every brief names the head to branch from first. A brief asks for no shell wait-loop: a spirit runs each command in the foreground and waits on its exit.
* ''The operator's hands'': the re-founding's walk on an attached TTY (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook#/the-three-face-rite`>>), the merge to `main` and the branch move to `develop`; before `M3`, the household domain and its DNS-01 credentials.
* ''Recurring faults to watch'': `browser-weld-witness` C4.2's reload on a cold dev cache; a browser vessel's 40 to 50 s boot to `live`, which CI's walk bounds must carry; the tw5 `carrier-shape` load timeout under the whole suite.
* ''The sensorium rests'' until the fully founded Lararium proof (operator ruling, 2026-10-27).
* ''Planning lives in canon'': a ruling lands in the road's `#/rulings`, an owed item in the road's #/the-leaps or its stride, a lane's design in its pono carrier and session state in this section; scratch keeps transient machine output and a lane's live state while it runs (operator, 2026-11-05, and 2026-11-12: //"While we have been working from a scratch dir with md files recently, this all belongs in the DreamNet and DreamDom design memes and roadmap/backlog memes. Find pono places to update/add this talked out context."//). About ninety feedback memories wait for a retirement o-takiage after standing.
* ''The operator's own keeper founding'' runs after the road's work lands, by hand, on a TTY; no spirit founds it, and no spirit stands a hearth.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-live-handoff`>>

!! The live handoff ~ two sessions, one seat, passed by word

A session can close and a fresh one boot from carriers; a ''live'' handoff lets both stand for a while, so the new seat can ask the old one what no carrier caught. The seat passes by explicit word, never by drift.

# ''One controller at a time.'' Only the seat-holder pushes the feature branch, fills lane slots and commits to the roadmap. The other session reads, asks, and works only lanes the seat-holder hands it by name.
# ''The outgoing seat trues #/state'' in this carrier, commits it, and names its lanes in flight: which will finish under it, and which pass to the incoming seat with their resume sheets.
# ''The incoming seat reads first'': the boot seed, the roadmap's cold start, and this carrier whole, #/the-lane-law included. Then it asks the outgoing seat, by session name over the message channel, whatever stays unclear: a lane's intent, a house reading's reason, a red that looked like a flake.
# ''The seat passes by message.'' The outgoing seat sends "the seat passes to <session>" once no push stands in flight and every lane it keeps has a named owner; the incoming seat answers "the seat holds". Until that answer, the outgoing seat still holds it.
# ''Lanes keep their spirit.'' A running lane answers the session that launched it. A lane that passes gets a new lead launched by the incoming seat from its resume sheet, never a message to a subagent of another session.
# ''Questions flow both ways after the pass''; the outgoing session may keep a lane to finish, and reports its handback to the new seat-holder as a lane would.

